WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:06.919
Let's talk today for a while about how governments
can and should deal with recessions.

00:00:06.919 --> 00:00:08.560
This is an important question right now.

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:10.650
The unemployment rate is skyrocketing.

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:16.360
More than 20 million people, 20, 24, 23 million
people have lost their jobs in the last five

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:17.360
weeks.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:23.090
We learned today that GDP was down almost
5 percent in the first quarter of the year.

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:28.160
We are seeing some ideas that second quarter
GDP could be down as much as 20 percent or

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:29.690
even more.

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:34.370
So we have a serious situation here, recession,
depression.

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:38.829
And we need to think about what are the options
for what governments can do.

00:00:38.829 --> 00:00:42.760
What has Trump done and is he doing a good
job or isn't he doing a good job?

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:45.720
And what I want to suggest to you today.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:48.550
Is that given the circumstances?

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:54.379
Donald Trump has actually used most of the
correct measures that are available under

00:00:54.379 --> 00:00:58.780
the paradigm of Keynesian economics to deal
with the recession.

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:04.519
But he's done it in a completely incompetent
crony and slow way.

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:11.390
And these are policies that are direct contradictions
to what Republicans claim are the policies

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:13.880
that a government should institute.

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:16.130
So there's also an element of hypocrisy.

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:17.630
But we'll get to that.

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:19.189
So to start with.

00:01:19.189 --> 00:01:25.110
Governments have two major sets of sort of
tools at their disposal, monetary policy and

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:26.640
fiscal policy.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:33.230
And very simply, when we say fiscal policy,
we mean government spending to influence the

00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:34.240
economy.

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:38.130
And when we say monetary policy, we're talking
about treasury management.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:43.380
In our case, it's whoever manages the currency,
treasury management of the money supply and

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:44.380
of interest rates.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:48.789
So fiscal policy would include things like
spending on infrastructure during a recession

00:01:48.789 --> 00:01:54.820
to create jobs, hire businesses to fix bridges,
the businesses, pay those employees that are

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:56.120
doing the work.

00:01:56.120 --> 00:02:01.890
Those employees then go and spend that money
in the economy, helping the grocery store

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:05.909
and the landscaping company then hire and
they hire and so on and so forth.

00:02:05.909 --> 00:02:07.890
So it's fiscal policy.

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:09.550
Then you've got monetary policy.

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:14.900
Monetary policy would be, for example, cutting
interest rates to encourage loans, making

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:21.180
loans cheaper so that people will go and borrow
money with which they can pay for expenses,

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:24.570
buy things, maybe pay employees, et cetera.

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:29.360
When Corona virus hits, businesses start to
close.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:30.440
People stop traveling.

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:36.050
A bunch of things happen without government
into an intervention that aren't good.

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:37.370
This increases unemployment.

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:40.780
Naturally, there is a demand collapse.

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:43.140
People stopped going to restaurants.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:45.590
So the restaurant no longer needs their workers.

00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:49.700
So they lay them off, which means the restaurant
owner isn't making any money.

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:52.730
They can't pay their rent or maybe they can't
pay their utilities.

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:54.450
So that affects their landlord.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:57.700
The restaurant doesn't have customers, so
it doesn't need to buy food.

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:00.060
So they stop spending with the food supplier.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:03.260
The food supplier doesn't need to buy as much
stuff from farmers.

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:05.390
The farmers no longer need as many employees.

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:07.510
I think you can sort of understand that.

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:14.190
Now, the food example is a more interesting
one because you see a demand reduction at

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:16.790
restaurants, but an increase at grocery stores.

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:22.450
But as we've learned with toilet paper, oftentimes
the personal and commercial supply chains

00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:25.440
for some of these goods are separate.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:26.880
So that's sort of a detail.

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:30.160
But I think you generally understand the the
idea here.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:35.680
The other thing that can happen in a recession
is that you get a deflationary spiral.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:41.020
So in the same way that if everybody wants
hand sanitizer, the price of sanitizer gets

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:44.850
driven up, up, up, which we saw about seven,
eight weeks ago.

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:51.770
If, for example, nobody's driving around,
this leads to a drop in oil and gasoline prices,

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:52.960
which we've seen now.

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:55.840
We may not care that much about oil prices
being low.

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:58.930
It sounds intuitive that lower prices are
just good, right?

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:02.720
I mean, if we're in a recession and stuff
costs less, that's good.

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:04.960
It means people can afford more of it.

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:10.400
The problem is that it can cause more unemployment
if you run a business.

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:16.410
And suddenly not only do people want less
of your stuff, but you have to lower the price

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:19.680
because people aren't willing to pay as much
for it.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:22.650
That further is not being able to keep employees
on.

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:27.650
So you lay people off, which means they don't
have money to buy stuff, which means the deflation

00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:28.650
continues.

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:32.840
That's why it's called a spiral in a healthy
economy.

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:36.980
You might see some deflation because we get
better at making something.

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:42.870
So if we become much more efficient at making
a particular product, we lower our cost to

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:45.220
make each one of those items.

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:49.460
And as companies compete with each other,
whoever can increase productivity and lower

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:52.500
costs can maybe gain more market share.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:53.500
So that's OK.

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:56.560
That's a good example of prices coming down.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:00.990
Deflation deflationary spirals in a recession
aren't good.

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:06.520
So let's now look at the things Donald Trump
has done or things that that have been done,

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:08.430
if not by Donald Trump personally.

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:09.990
So the Fed cut interest rates.

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:10.990
Great.

00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:12.320
That's monetary policy.

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:14.600
That's the right thing to do.

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:21.040
The Treasury has created these P.P. programs,
paycheck protection plan programs where the

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:23.570
interest rate is its effect, effectively negative.

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:24.570
You get money.

00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:29.690
And as long as you use it for the right things,
the loan doesn't have to be paid back in full.

00:05:29.690 --> 00:05:31.650
That's the correct thing to do.

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:37.730
Now, the problem is they did it in the crony
capitalist way that Trump always seems to

00:05:37.730 --> 00:05:38.730
do.

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:39.730
It took too long.

00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:42.200
There were all sorts of logistical failures.

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:44.690
The system didn't actually start distributing.

00:05:44.690 --> 00:05:47.560
Money for a month or longer.

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:48.770
Money ran out too quickly.

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:54.090
A lot of the money went to really large corporations
and other institutions instead of small businesses.

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:56.920
Small businesses ended up not being able to
get money.

00:05:56.920 --> 00:05:58.460
Trump has to scramble.

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:00.970
Ask for more money and ask for money back.

00:06:00.970 --> 00:06:02.810
Total disaster in its implementation.

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:05.830
But it is policy that makes sense.

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:10.190
Now there's a practical way in which peopIe
is kind of a blend of monetary and fiscal

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:11.590
policy.

00:06:11.590 --> 00:06:18.220
It's sort of like low interest loans, which
is monetary, but forgivable loans to business

00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:23.180
are sort of like government spending, which
is fiscal policy, but that's kind of a detail

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:24.630
in terminology.

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:31.580
Another important monetary policy component
is expectations management.

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:37.260
This is part of why, remember, we talked last
week or the week before we get bad unemployment

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:38.260
news.

00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:40.080
And yet the stock market sometimes goes up.

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:41.630
What's going on?

00:06:41.630 --> 00:06:45.820
Managing expectations is part of monetary
policy.

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:53.070
You tell the public in advance what you expect
to see happen so that it more smoothly.

00:06:53.070 --> 00:06:54.120
This is the goal, right?

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:57.440
It more smoothly gets priced into the markets.

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:01.990
And then when the news hits, it's not a shock
to the system because the markets were expecting

00:07:01.990 --> 00:07:03.290
it anyway.

00:07:03.290 --> 00:07:06.080
That is a form of monetary policy.

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:07.320
Expectations management.

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:10.400
So let's now talk about fiscal stimulus.

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:12.580
You can do fiscal stimulus by cutting taxes.

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:14.330
So when Trump proposed, that didn't happen.

00:07:14.330 --> 00:07:21.150
But Trump proposed what if we do a waiver
or a moratorium on the payroll tax until the

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:22.650
end of the year?

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:26.050
That would be a form of fiscal stimulus through
a tax cut.

00:07:26.050 --> 00:07:29.450
You can also do fiscal stimulus by the government
spending money.

00:07:29.450 --> 00:07:30.790
This can be buying things.

00:07:30.790 --> 00:07:33.120
This can be hiring companies to do stuff.

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:37.199
It can also be just the government sending
people checks could be a one time payment,

00:07:37.199 --> 00:07:41.260
which we've seen could be by increasing unemployment
benefits.

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:43.980
What we're talking about is deficit spending.

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:49.470
There are sort of people on the right who
like the cartoon ify this as printing money.

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:52.340
We talked last week with Stephanie Kelton
about that.

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:55.240
Calling it printing money is not really an
accurate term.

00:07:55.240 --> 00:07:57.030
What we're talking about is deficit spending.

00:07:57.030 --> 00:08:00.310
We're talking about digitally adding money
to people's accounts.

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:02.949
Now, again, Trump is doing this.

00:08:02.949 --> 00:08:05.600
He's boosting unemployment benefits.

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:07.400
That's fiscal policy.

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:12.830
It's getting money to people which people
can then spend increasing aggregate demand,

00:08:12.830 --> 00:08:17.250
maintain prices relatively level more level
than they otherwise would be.

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:21.430
When Trump cuts twelve hundred dollar checks,
really the Treasury does.

00:08:21.430 --> 00:08:23.540
That's fiscal policy.

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:24.840
It's government spending.

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:29.970
Now, they also sent billions to some industries,
airlines, cruise companies.

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:33.500
That's also fiscal stimulus and it is the
right tool.

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:37.610
But the problem has been, again, implementation
took way too long.

00:08:37.610 --> 00:08:41.270
Not nearly enough money for the average individual.

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:43.839
A ton of the spending was done with companies.

00:08:43.839 --> 00:08:45.440
That really shouldn't be the priority.

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:47.910
Should've been more money for individuals.

00:08:47.910 --> 00:08:53.410
We heard last week from billionaire investor
Tomas Polish Pedia who said that, you know,

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:56.850
some of the companies being bailed out, these
are really bailing out.

00:08:56.850 --> 00:09:00.839
These are bailouts of the billionaires who
are speculating, not the employees.

00:09:00.839 --> 00:09:04.699
If you really want to bail out employees,
you can do it in a much more direct way.

00:09:04.699 --> 00:09:08.800
In addition, Trump has done it in a completely
crony capitalist way.

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:13.620
He's reduced oversight, which has turned some
of these bailout funds into essentially slush

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:14.620
funds.

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:18.449
He's used government money to buy things like
ventilators and PPE.

00:09:18.449 --> 00:09:19.959
That's stimulus.

00:09:19.959 --> 00:09:24.279
Except there's always something weird when
Trump does it, like the company that is bankrupt

00:09:24.279 --> 00:09:28.860
with no employees, which has gotten a big
contract to essentially just middleman the

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:32.060
purchase of a bunch of masks way above market
value.

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:34.129
So it's the right tool.

00:09:34.129 --> 00:09:36.500
It's being done in a crony capitalist way.

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:38.300
The focus is often wrong.

00:09:38.300 --> 00:09:39.639
It took forever.

00:09:39.639 --> 00:09:40.910
Millions have still got nothing.

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:45.730
The you know, the unemployment insurance supplements
have still not been received by a lot of people.

00:09:45.730 --> 00:09:51.560
So in short, Donald Trump has used the right
tools under the Keynesian paradigm.

00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:53.820
And I'll talk more about that in a second.

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:55.950
Fiscal policy, monetary policy.

00:09:55.950 --> 00:09:58.050
But he's done it in an incompetent way.

00:09:58.050 --> 00:10:01.899
And it, of course, is, of course, massive
hypocrisy, because what we hear from the right

00:10:01.899 --> 00:10:05.059
year in and year out is, one, we don't have
the money for this.

00:10:05.059 --> 00:10:06.059
That's clearly untrue.

00:10:06.059 --> 00:10:07.059
We found it right.

00:10:07.059 --> 00:10:08.779
And again, it's not really finding it.

00:10:08.779 --> 00:10:09.779
That's a misnomer.

00:10:09.779 --> 00:10:14.379
But we were able to do it despite Republicans
saying it's not possible.

00:10:14.379 --> 00:10:17.910
So there's the hip, hip hypocrisy element.

00:10:17.910 --> 00:10:23.240
And number two, you often hear Republicans
saying that these types of techniques don't

00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:24.309
actually work.

00:10:24.309 --> 00:10:26.660
And yet here they are saying we've actually
got to do it.

00:10:26.660 --> 00:10:31.119
So incompetence and hypocrisy, but the right
tools have been used.

00:10:31.119 --> 00:10:35.920
Now, what hasn't Trump done that he could
have done infrastructure spending? Trump has

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:38.499
talked about doing infrastructure spending.

00:10:38.499 --> 00:10:43.230
It would be important to do six solid months
of infrastructure spending.

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:44.500
So have the government.

00:10:44.500 --> 00:10:50.649
Spend on fixing roads, bridges, tunnels, the
electric grid could be bolstered.

00:10:50.649 --> 00:10:53.279
Water supply could be better protected.

00:10:53.279 --> 00:10:55.329
There's tons of things that could be done.

00:10:55.329 --> 00:10:56.730
They would boost employment.

00:10:56.730 --> 00:10:58.660
They would inject money.

00:10:58.660 --> 00:11:01.819
And when we come out of this, whenever it
is, we would be better prepared.

00:11:01.819 --> 00:11:06.779
We would have better infrastructure to resume
all of that economic activity.

00:11:06.779 --> 00:11:08.230
It's not been done.

00:11:08.230 --> 00:11:09.430
It's time to do it.

00:11:09.430 --> 00:11:11.140
Trump should have already gotten this going.

00:11:11.140 --> 00:11:12.140
He talks.

00:11:12.140 --> 00:11:13.940
Maybe it'll happen in the next phase.

00:11:13.940 --> 00:11:14.940
We don't know.

00:11:14.940 --> 00:11:16.810
That certainly has been a mistake.

00:11:16.810 --> 00:11:19.230
Now, let's zoom out from all of this.

00:11:19.230 --> 00:11:20.250
Why?

00:11:20.250 --> 00:11:25.199
Everything I've been talking about, these
fiscal policy tools, these are what we call

00:11:25.199 --> 00:11:27.170
Keynesian tools.

00:11:27.170 --> 00:11:32.279
They're from the School of Fiscal Policy founder
John Maynard Keynes.

00:11:32.279 --> 00:11:37.790
Our government has these tools available instead
of other tools, because Keynesian economics

00:11:37.790 --> 00:11:40.249
are sort of the orthodoxy.

00:11:40.249 --> 00:11:46.769
And this is based on you see a recession fundamentally
as a demand shortage.

00:11:46.769 --> 00:11:53.430
And you then take these reactions of fiscal
policy that we've described.

00:11:53.430 --> 00:12:00.610
That is not the only way to see the role of
government before and during a recession or

00:12:00.610 --> 00:12:01.610
depression.

00:12:01.610 --> 00:12:06.429
A more progressive economic worldview would
say, all right, David, you accurately described

00:12:06.429 --> 00:12:10.639
the Keynesian tools, but those tools are not
proactive.

00:12:10.639 --> 00:12:11.970
They are reactive.

00:12:11.970 --> 00:12:17.339
So I'll give you some examples of what a more
progressive, proactive perspective would be.

00:12:17.339 --> 00:12:22.339
A more progressive economic perspective would
say the federal government should always be

00:12:22.339 --> 00:12:25.160
bolstering state unemployment insurance.

00:12:25.160 --> 00:12:29.519
So if they did that all along and people got
more money from unemployment insurance, if

00:12:29.519 --> 00:12:34.029
more money was available at the state level,
then you wouldn't see this panicked scramble

00:12:34.029 --> 00:12:35.040
to do it now.

00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:38.649
But you wouldn't see the decrease in demand
that you are seeing now because people don't

00:12:38.649 --> 00:12:39.649
have money.

00:12:39.649 --> 00:12:45.040
Another perspective, the federal government
should always be funding more education and

00:12:45.040 --> 00:12:51.029
other government jobs so that when you have
a recession, a lot of those jobs just continue

00:12:51.029 --> 00:12:54.750
and you avoid having to bail out as many companies
with loans.

00:12:54.750 --> 00:12:57.000
You avoid having to send as many people.

00:12:57.000 --> 00:13:00.880
Twelve hundred dollar checks because a lot
of these government jobs, including in education,

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:01.880
can simply continue.

00:13:01.880 --> 00:13:06.429
Now, there's a that's that's a political perspective
that right wingers often disagree with.

00:13:06.429 --> 00:13:11.389
Others would say if we had permanent universal
basic income, we would obviate the need for

00:13:11.389 --> 00:13:16.269
as much of this desperate spending because
people would already be getting that baseline

00:13:16.269 --> 00:13:18.259
income from the government.

00:13:18.259 --> 00:13:22.029
And thus this pandemic recession would be
less of an emergency.

00:13:22.029 --> 00:13:25.800
It would still be one, but it would be less
of an emergency if we had.

00:13:25.800 --> 00:13:27.709
Here we go now.

00:13:27.709 --> 00:13:29.249
Government run health care.

00:13:29.249 --> 00:13:34.189
You wouldn't need Trump negotiating with insurance
companies to waive co-pays on corona virus

00:13:34.189 --> 00:13:35.550
testing or treatment.

00:13:35.550 --> 00:13:39.019
It would simply be done because the government
is more involved in health care.

00:13:39.019 --> 00:13:42.199
So that exceeds our time today.

00:13:42.199 --> 00:13:47.559
But the seed I'm planting is Trump has used
the right tools given the system we have.

00:13:47.559 --> 00:13:52.779
He's used them incompetently, but there are
more progressive economic perspectives which

00:13:52.779 --> 00:13:58.230
would say if we had much more government involvement
all along in employment, in fiscal policy

00:13:58.230 --> 00:14:03.490
all the time, it would prevent as much of
an emergency need for that to be done right

00:14:03.490 --> 00:14:04.490
now.

00:14:04.490 --> 00:14:08.639
Many people don't want more government involvement
and that's a political debate.

00:14:08.639 --> 00:14:10.870
So I hope that this is useful to people.

00:14:10.870 --> 00:14:13.350
There's been a clamoring for more policy stuff
like this.

00:14:13.350 --> 00:14:15.290
I'll produce what the people want.

00:14:15.290 --> 00:14:16.339
